{
  "dom_id": "step-q330934",
  "name": "Question 11",
  "problem": "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $Z∼N(0,1),$ compute $P(-2.20<Z<2.10).$\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-2.2\\,$ | $\\,0.0139\\,$ | $\\,0.0143\\,$ | $\\,0.0146\\,$ |\n| $\\,-2.1\\,$ | $\\,0.0179\\,$ | $\\,0.0183\\,$ | $\\,0.0188\\,$ |\n| $\\,-2.0\\,$ | $\\,0.0228\\,$ | $\\,0.0232\\,$ | $\\,0.0239\\,$ |\n\n$P(-2.20<Z<2.10)=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "We're given that $Z∼N(0,1),$ and we wish to compute $P(-2.20<Z<2.10).$ The required probability is represented by the area shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14259374/assets/3c11b52aec858cf87417265a020e446875193a24d44b202f06d2f0b127d4b559.png)\n\nBy the definition of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z),$\n\n$$\nP(Z\\le z)=Φ(z).\n$$\n\nSo, we express the required probability in terms of $Φ(z)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-2.20<Z<2.10) & =P(Z<2.10)-P(Z\\le -2.20) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 2.10)-P(Z\\le -2.20) \\\\  & =Φ(2.10)-Φ(-2.20)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(-2.20) & =0.0139, \\\\ Φ(-2.10) & =0.0179,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nbut we are not given $Φ(2.10).$ However, we can compute this using symmetry:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(2.10) & =P(Z\\le 2.10) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le -2.10) \\\\  & =1-Φ(-2.10) \\\\  & =1-0.0179 \\\\  & =0.9821\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-2.20<Z<2.10) & =Φ(2.10)-Φ(-2.20) \\\\  & =0.9821-0.0139 \\\\  & =0.9682.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
